From: Vladimir V. Kondratyev <vladimirkondratyev2@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: btintel_pcie: fix s2idle suspend abort (-EBUSY)

s2idle suspend aborts repeatedly on Intel BT PCIe controllers:
btintel_pcie_suspend() writes the D3 (sleep) state and waits up to 200 ms
for the controller's "GP0 alive" doorbell interrupt. After
BTINTEL_PCIE_DX_TRANSITION_MAX_RETRIES timeouts the fallback re-checks
controller state via btintel_pcie_in_d3()/in_d0(), which read
boot_stage_cache -- a field only updated by the interrupt handler. When
the alive interrupt is dropped (a controller quirk), the cache is stale,
the fallback wrongly sees "still in D0", all retries fail, and the driver
returns -EBUSY (-16), aborting the whole suspend. logind then retries
every ~30 s, keeping the laptop awake in a closed lid.

Also clear the GP0 cause with a direct write-1-to-clear write:
MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES is W1C, and the read-modify-write helper writes zero
to GP0, leaving the cause uncleared and acknowledging unrelated pending
causes through the read value.

Backports two upstream commits:
  Bluetooth: btintel_pcie: fix stale cache in set_dxstate fallback check
  Bluetooth: btintel_pcie: clear the GP0 cause with a W1C write

Upstream-Status: Backport [12997a385f7140c66f733128b76845fd906c270a, f2cee6e2aa35a6a701ce3f4bedf20273c808361f]
Carried by Dakota against v7.3.3-ogc1; drop this patch once both upstream
commits are backported to the OGC tree or the Linux 7.3 stable series.
---
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btintel_pcie.c
@@ -3200,15 +3200,19 @@
 			   "Timeout (%u ms) on alive interrupt for D%d entry, retry count %d",
 			   dx_intr_timeout_ms, dxstate, retry);
 
-		/* clear gp0 cause */
-		btintel_pcie_clr_reg_bits(data,
-					  BTINTEL_PCIE_CSR_MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES,
-					  BTINTEL_PCIE_MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES_GP0);
+		/* MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES is W1C. Write only GP0 so other
+		 * pending causes are not acknowledged here.
+		 */
+		btintel_pcie_wr_reg32(data,
+				      BTINTEL_PCIE_CSR_MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES,
+				      BTINTEL_PCIE_MSIX_HW_INT_CAUSES_GP0);
 
-		/* A hardware bug may cause the alive interrupt to be missed.
-		 * Check if the controller reached the expected state and retry
-		 * the operation only if it hasn't.
+		/* A hardware bug may cause the alive interrupt to be missed. Refresh
+		 * boot_stage_cache from hardware, since only the interrupt handler
+		 * updates it. Finally retry only if the state check still fails.
 		 */
+		data->boot_stage_cache = btintel_pcie_rd_reg32(data,
+						       BTINTEL_PCIE_CSR_BOOT_STAGE_REG);
 		if (dxstate == BTINTEL_PCIE_STATE_D0) {
 			if (btintel_pcie_in_d0(data))
 				return 0;
